  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Gymnopus iocephalus is a small, delicate mushroom belonging to the Omphalotaceae family, characterized by its thin, fragile fruiting bodies. This species is saprobic, feeding on decaying wood and plant matter in forested ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap appearance",
      "description": "Small, thin cap (typically 5-15mm) with a distinctive pale to whitish coloration, often with a slightly umbilicate or depressed center",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Stalk structure",
      "description": "Very thin, wiry, and fragile dark-colored stem that contrasts sharply with the pale cap",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill attachment",
      "description": "Gills are decurrent to adnate, relatively distant, and pale in color, running down the stalk",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Gymnopus iocephalus is found in moist, shaded deciduous and mixed forests throughout New England, growing on dead wood, leaf litter, and decaying plant debris.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Members of the Gymnopus genus are sometimes called 'pinhead mushrooms' due to their diminutive size, and despite their fragility, they are among the most persistent decomposers in forest ecosystems.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
